<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">António de Oliveira Salazar</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">António de Oliveira Salazar was the authoritarian leader of Portugal from 1932 to 1968, heading the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estado-novo-portugal">Estado Novo</a> regime. He insisted on keeping Portugal's African colonies, treating them as overseas provinces. His refusal to decolonise led to long colonial wars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/angola">Angola</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guinea">Guinea</a>, which contributed to the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a> after his regime fell in 1974.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/antonio-de-oliveira-salazar.jpg" alt="António de Oliveira Salazar" width="315" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Manuel Alves de San Payo · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Ant%C3%B3nio_de_Oliveira_Salazar_portrait_(by_Manuel_Alves_San_Payo)_%E2%80%93_Lisboa.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
